<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
        <artifactId>vexelray</artifactId>
        <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>vexelray-technique-sdf</artifactId>

    <description>
        The SDF ray-march technique. Takes an SdfScene — a Surface, a Shading model, march settings, a camera —
        and composes the fullscreen vertex+fragment pair that renders it, as SupirVast `core` IR lowered to
        SPIR-V. This is the ShaderComposer seat that vexelray-shader has been reserving for "an SDF composer
        turns a signed-distance scene into a fullscreen fragment".

        The scene is a record all the way down, so ShaderKey collapses equal scenes onto one compiled shader
        set with no bespoke fingerprinting, and the ShaderCache serves them.

        Not yet a RenderTechnique: the SPI's runtime (Phase 2) does not exist. What is here is the part that
        does not depend on it — composition — and the module is named for what it becomes.
    </description>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- The core-IR authoring vocabulary (Ir). Reached transitively too, but declared because it is used. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
            <artifactId>vexelray-ir</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <!-- Surfaces as data, and their lowering to a marchable distance field. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
            <artifactId>vexelray-surface</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <!-- So a technique can say when it has been handed something it cannot render. See ConeField.compose. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
            <artifactId>vexelray-diagnostics</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <!-- ShaderComposer/ComposedShader/ShaderKey/ShaderCache, and the Shading half of the lighting model. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
            <artifactId>vexelray-shader</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <!-- SupirVast core IR, plus the Fullscreen vertex primitive the march draws over. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.supirvast</groupId>
            <artifactId>vastir</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.supirvast</groupId>
            <artifactId>vastir-tools</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          The runtime this technique plugs into. Two edges arrive together and neither is avoidable:
          vexelray-engine-api for the RenderTechnique SPI, and vexelray-engine for VulkanTechniqueContext, which
          is the Vulkan-bearing subtype a technique must cast to in order to reach a device and build a pipeline
          (docs/refactor-decisions.md D3).

          Depending on the engine implementation is the cost of the deferred backend abstraction, paid where it
          is visible. The alternative — putting VulkanTechniqueContext in vexelray-vulkan — would make the
          substrate depend on the public API above it, which is worse.

          This is also why the march smokes moved here from vexelray-vulkan's test scope: the substrate cannot
          test-depend on a technique that compile-depends on the substrate, and the pom comment that used to sit
          on that dependency was already right about why it should not.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
            <artifactId>vexelray-engine-api</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
            <artifactId>vexelray-engine</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Test-only: the march smokes render headlessly and count pixels. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
            <artifactId>vexelray-os-api</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <profiles>
        <!-- The march smokes open a device, so the host platform's provider is needed at test runtime. -->
        <profile>
            <id>platform-windows</id>
            <activation>
                <os><family>windows</family></os>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
                    <artifactId>vexelray-os-windows</artifactId>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
        </profile>
        <profile>
            <id>platform-linux</id>
            <activation>
                <os><family>unix</family><name>linux</name></os>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
                    <artifactId>vexelray-os-linux</artifactId>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
        </profile>
        <profile>
            <id>platform-macos</id>
            <activation>
                <os><family>mac</family></os>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
                    <artifactId>vexelray-os-macos</artifactId>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
        </profile>
    </profiles>
</project>
